      "text": "I now have a solid foundation of verified information from the metadata summary, Wikipedia, and confirmed author biography details. Here is a comprehensive write-up you can use for your book blog review:\n\n---\n\n# 📚 *People We Meet on Vacation* by Emily Henry — A Review Resource\n\n## 🗂️ Plot Summary\n\nPublished in **2021 by Berkley**, *People We Meet on Vacation* is a dual-timeline contemporary romance of approximately **94,000 words**. The story centers on **Alex and Poppy**, two best friends with seemingly nothing in common who have taken an annual vacation together every summer for a decade. The novel weaves between two timelines: the **present (\"now\")**, in which Poppy is trying to repair a friendship that mysteriously fell apart, and **the past (\"two years ago\")**, in which readers slowly discover what went wrong between them.\n\nPoppy is a travel writer living her dream in New York City — but secretly unfulfilled. Alex is an English teacher in their quiet hometown of Linfield, Ohio, content in a life that Poppy once fled. Their friendship is the emotional heartbeat of the novel, and the slow-burn romance that emerges from it is built on years of shared history, inside jokes, and deeply buried feelings. The structure is masterful: every chapter in the past recontextualizes the present, building to a revelation about the night everything broke.\n\n---\n\n## 🎭 Key Themes\n\n- **Friendship-to-romance (\"friends to lovers\"):** The romantic arc grows organically from a decade-long bond, making the emotional payoff feel genuinely earned rather than rushed.\n- **Dual timelines:** The alternating structure creates sustained tension and narrative mystery — readers know something went wrong before they know *what*, which keeps pages turning.\n- **Grief and regret:** Both characters are haunted by choices made and paths not taken. The novel treats emotional avoidance and self-sabotage with real sensitivity.\n- **Self-discovery:** Poppy's arc is largely about reconciling the life she thought she wanted with the life that would actually make her happy.\n- **Second chances:** At its core, this is a story about whether love and friendship — once broken — can truly be rebuilt.\n\n---\n\n## 📣 Critical Reception\n\nThe search results returned limited granular data from specific named outlets, but here is what is confirmed and well-established:\n\n- The novel was **widely reviewed** upon its May 2021 publication and became a **#1 New York Times Bestseller**, cementing Emily Henry's status as a leading voice in contemporary romance.\n- It is celebrated by critics and readers alike for its **dual-timeline structure, witty banter, and emotional depth** — qualities that distinguish it from more lightweight beach reads.\n- The book garnered a massive **Goodreads following**, with hundreds of thousands of ratings, and is consistently praised for making readers cry — a testament to the emotional stakes Henry builds over the course of the novel.\n- General critical consensus highlights Henry's ability to write romance that takes *friendship* as seriously as romantic love, which reviewers found refreshing and emotionally sophisticated.\n\n> ⚠️ **Note for your blog:** For specific star ratings, blurbs from named outlets (e.g., *Entertainment Weekly*, *NPR*, *The New York Times*), or exact Goodreads figures, I'd recommend visiting [Goodreads directly](https://www.goodreads.com) or checking the publisher's press page, as the search tools available did not return granular outlet-level data. These are worth citing for added credibility in your review.\n\n---\n\n## 📖 Emily Henry's Broader Body of Work\n\nEmily Henry is a **#1 New York Times bestselling** author known for contemporary romances that balance wit, emotional intelligence, and literary craft. Her confirmed bibliography includes:\n\n| Book | Year |\n|------|------|\n| *Beach Read* | 2020 |\n| *People We Meet on Vacation* | 2021 |\n| *Book Lovers* | 2022 |\n| *Happy Place* | 2023 |\n\n### How *People We Meet on Vacation* fits in:\n- It is considered by many fans to be Henry's **most emotionally devastating** novel — more tearjerker than *Beach Read*, which leans more into rom-com territory.\n- Where **Beach Read** pairs two rival writers in a fun, high-concept enemies-to-lovers setup, *People We Meet on Vacation* is more **character-driven and melancholy**, with a slower-burn, deeper emotional current.\n- Critics and readers frequently compare the two as companion pieces in Henry's emerging style: **smart, self-aware romance with literary sensibility** — characters who are readers, writers, or otherwise intellectually oriented, and stories that ask hard questions about what people really want from their lives.\n- *Book Lovers* (2022) continued this trajectory, and reviewers noted Henry's consistent thematic interest in **women reconciling ambition with intimacy**.\n- Across her catalog, Henry is recognized for **prioritizing emotional realism** over fantasy escapism — her romances feel grounded because the obstacles are internal (fear, grief, self-doubt) rather than contrived external plot devices.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Tips for Your Blog Review\n\n1. **Lead with the structure** — the dual timeline is what sets this apart from most romance novels and gives you a lot to discuss analytically.\n2. **Explore the friendship as the real love story** — many reviewers argue that the platonic bond is as central as the romantic one, which is a rich angle.\n3. **Compare to *Beach Read*** — your readers will likely want to know which to read first or which they'll prefer based on mood.\n4. **Address the emotional gut-punch** — readers consistently cite unexpected emotional intensity; naming that upfront manages expectations well.\n\nGood luck with your review! 🌟",
